These tests require a disposable btrfs image mounted writable, root, and
Python 3.  They leave their files in place because unlink is not implemented.
Use a new test directory for each run.  For example, inside the test VM:

    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/namespace.py create /mnt/btrfs/ns
    umount /mnt/btrfs

On the host, while the image is unmounted:

    btrfs check --readonly --check-data-csum /path/to/image
    python3 regress/sys/btrfs/mirrors.py /path/to/image

mirrors.py supplements the checker by comparing every allocated metadata copy
and checking that no allocation overlaps a physical superblock stripe.
It supports the writer's SINGLE/DUP format.  The normal checker can recover
from a corrupt DUP copy and still return success, so use both checks.

Then in the VM:

    mount_btrfs /dev/sd1c /mnt/btrfs
    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/namespace.py verify /mnt/btrfs/ns
    umount /mnt/btrfs

flags.py uses create/verify phases and readonly after a read-only remount.
It covers nodump/immutable/append round trips, owner and nonowner permissions,
clearing flags, writes through existing descriptors, append writes, hard-link
aliases, directory policy, FIFO metadata, rejected flag masks, and kqueue
attribute notifications. Run the independent checks between mounts.

Run all driver-involving SSH commands under timeout.  Repeat with both 4 KiB
and 16 KiB metadata nodes to cover tree growth, and check the resulting image
with btrfs-progs.  The test covers concurrent creation in separate and shared
directories, hash collisions, long names, negative cache invalidation, inode
uniqueness, file data, sparse writes, fsync, permissions, owner/group/mode,
directory size/link accounting, nested parent lookup, and readdir.

Writable images must meet the feature restrictions in
sys/btrfs/WRITE_SUPPORT.md.  The host must not mount an untrusted image.

idle.py takes a new test directory and checks repeated fsync and mount sync
without pending changes, followed by further namespace and data mutation.
Run after writable remount of both a fresh image and one with a large extent
tree; sync must leave the mount writable.  Unmount and check independently.

symlink.py uses the same create/verify phases as namespace.py.  It covers
relative, absolute, dangling, chained and looping links, directory traversal,
short readlink buffers, maximum target/name lengths, hash collisions across
commits, permissions, concurrent creation, and directory accounting.  Run
both phases with an intervening unmount, independent check, and remount.

locks.py takes a new test directory and checks flock and POSIX record locks:
conflicts, shared locks, independent ranges, F_GETLK, partial unlock, SEEK_END,
blocking wakeup, duplicate descriptors, symlink aliases, and release on close
and process exit.  Run it under timeout, then unmount and check the image.
Its `readonly existing-test-directory` mode tests locks after a read-only
remount.  `reclaim mountpoint` creates two files and forcibly unmounts the
disposable filesystem with held locks and blocked waiters; run it from outside
the mount.  This mode checks that vnode reclaim interrupts the waiters.

kqueue.py uses `create new-test-directory` to test read/write readiness, EOF,
poll/select, vnode write/extend/attribute events, directory notifications,
subscription masks, descriptor close, and a reader woken by a child writer.
Use `readonly existing-test-directory` after a read-only remount.
`revoke mountpoint` forcibly unmounts with all three filters registered and
checks delivery and cleanup of revoke events.  Run this from outside the
mount under timeout, then check the unmounted image independently.

On a small disposable image, enospc.py fills the existing metadata block group
with empty files.  It checks that failed creation leaves no entry or parent
metadata changes, and that the filesystem remains writable and can sync.
For example:

    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/enospc.py /mnt/btrfs/full

Unmount and run the independent filesystem checker after this test as well.

hardlink.py uses create-extended/verify phases and a readonly phase after a
read-only remount.  It checks shared inode identity, data, attributes and
advisory locks, link counts, symlink aliases, concurrent links, hash collisions,
notifications, permissions, and extended inode-reference overflow, including
collisions across commits.  On images made with `mkfs.btrfs -O ^extref`, use
create instead of create-extended to check failure atomicity when a packed
inode-reference item fills.
The limit/verify-limit phases grow one inode to the advertised `LINK_MAX`,
check every alias after remount, and ensure the next link fails atomically.
Run limit in two separate directories on the same image to exercise metadata
allocation past the 64 MiB superblock mirror, then unmount and run both host
checks.  Writable remount and idle sync/unmount also exercise commits without
pending namespace mutations.

lookup.py checks hash lookup of independently imported directory items.  On
the host, create a fixture and import it into an unmounted disposable image:

    python3 regress/sys/btrfs/lookup.py seed /tmp/btrfs-lookup
    mkfs.btrfs -f -n 4096 -s 4096 -m dup -d single \
        -O ^free-space-tree,^block-group-tree \
        --rootdir /tmp/btrfs-lookup --subvol rw:subvol /path/to/image

In the VM, mount the image and run:

    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/lookup.py verify /mnt/btrfs

Repeat after read-only remount.  This covers packed hash collisions, long and
UTF-8 names, hard links, symlinks, missing names, subvolume entries and parent
traversal.

subvol.py tests simultaneous writable mounts of disjoint subvolume hierarchies.
Seed an unmounted image on the host:

    python3 regress/sys/btrfs/subvol.py seed /tmp/btrfs-subvol
    mkfs.btrfs -f -b 1G -n 4096 -s 4096 -m dup -d single \
        -O ^free-space-tree,^block-group-tree --rootdir /tmp/btrfs-subvol \
        --subvol rw:left --subvol rw:left/nested \
        --subvol ro:left/frozen --subvol rw:right /path/to/image

Read IDs from `btrfs inspect-internal dump-tree -t root /path/to/image`.
In the VM, with the image initially unmounted, supply the IDs in that order:

    python3 /mnt/src/regress/sys/btrfs/subvol.py exercise \
        /dev/sd1c /mnt/subvol-test 256 257 258 259

This mounts and unmounts its own views. It covers concurrent writes in three
trees, stat identity, parent/getcwd traversal, cross-tree hard-link rejection,
read-only tree and mount policy, overlap rejection in both directions, invalid
IDs, busy and forced unmount, surviving views, last-view teardown, and racing
overlapping attaches. Run both independent checks with all views unmounted,
then mount the top-level tree read-only and run `subvol.py verify mountpoint`.
Repeat with 16 KiB metadata. Rebuild mount_btrfs with the matching sys/mount.h
when changing kernels: its mount arguments now include the selected tree ID.

ipc.py uses create/verify phases and a readonly phase after a read-only
remount. It covers FIFO and Unix-domain socket creation, hard-link aliases,
blocking/nonblocking pipe I/O, EOF, kqueue readiness, socket connections,
permissions, hash collisions, directory accounting, and unsupported device
creation. Run the independent checks between mounts as above.

inline.py uses seed on the host to prepare a rootdir fixture, write in the VM,
then verify after unmount/check/remount. It covers conversion of imported
uncompressed inline files, partial overwrites, append, sector boundaries,
sparse extension, cached reads, concurrent writers, hard links, and repeated
COW after fsync. Its capacity phase fills existing data space on a small
image and checks that a failed sparse inline conversion preserves data and
attributes and leaves the mount writable. Unmount and check afterward.
Import a fresh seed image with --compress zlib and use reject to check
compressed-inline write rejection without attribute changes. Zlib reads are
not implemented, so unmount, use btrfs restore on the host, and run verify-seed
on the restored directory to check the unchanged data independently.
